A socket file is not a running daemon
=====================================

`quixi --capabilities` reported `input_synthesis: true` on this box while ydotoold was not running
and every synthesized event failed. The cause is `ydotool_socket()`: it probed candidates with
`Path::exists()`, and a unix socket leaves its inode behind when the process behind it dies. So
"a socket file is here" was being read as "something will answer", the capability report advertised
input, and the event went into a dead socket.

Measured before the fix, on the development box:

    $ pgrep ydotoold            # nothing
    $ ls -l /run/user/1000/.ydotool_socket
    srw------- 1 volkdude volkdude 0 Nov  1 17:22 ...   # yesterday's inode, still there
    $ connect() to it           # ECONNREFUSED
    $ quixi --capabilities | grep input_synthesis
      "input_synthesis": true,                          # a lie

`exists()` is now a connect. A local unix connect either completes or is refused immediately, so it
costs nothing, has no timeout to pick, and cannot claim a live daemon that is not there. Same
treatment for the `YDOTOOL_SOCKET` override, which had the identical flaw.

This edition's `cursor_position_is_not_claimed_until_known` had been relaxed to measure the move
rather than the capability report, so the suite was green in both states and could not catch this.
The new test binds a listener, drops it, and asserts the file survives while `socket_is_live()` says
no — the exact state that lied.

--- a/crates/quixi-platform/src/linux.rs	2026-11-02 07:05:37.218225113 -0800
+++ b/crates/quixi-platform/src/linux.rs	2026-11-02 07:08:18.948811620 -0800
@@ -1507,6 +1507,22 @@
 // Input synthesis (needs ydotool + a running ydotoold)
 // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 
+/// Is something actually accepting connections on this socket path?
+///
+/// A unix socket leaves its inode behind when the process behind it dies, so `exists()` answers
+/// "a socket file is here", not "a daemon will answer". Those are different claims, and confusing
+/// them is how the capability report ends up advertising input synthesis on a box where every
+/// synthesized event fails: `cursor_position_is_not_claimed_until_known` moves the pointer, asks
+/// whether the position is now known, and gets "it should be" from the probe and "no" from reality.
+///
+/// Connect, then drop. A local unix connect either completes or is refused immediately, so there is
+/// no round trip to wait on and no timeout to pick, and it cannot report a live daemon that is not
+/// there.
+fn socket_is_live(path: &std::path::Path) -> bool {
+    use std::os::unix::net::UnixStream;
+    UnixStream::connect(path).is_ok()
+}
